EXT. SALT FLAT, ABANDONED STAGE STOP - NOON

White ground. White sky. A dead well. Two hitching posts.

JEDEDIAH "JED" ARROWOOD (50s, long duster, one glove) sits on a crate. Whittles. Doesn't look up.

Hooves. Slow.

MARISOL QUINTANILLA (30s, black hat, rifle across the saddle) rides in. Stops thirty feet off.

He keeps whittling. She keeps still.

JED
Water's bad.

MARISOL
I brought my own.

JED
Figured.

She swings down. Boots crunch. She leaves the rifle on the horse.

He folds his knife. Slides it into his boot.

MARISOL
You're a long way from Dodge.

JED
So are you.

A hawk. A wind. Nothing else.

She walks toward him. Ten feet. Five.

MARISOL
Eleven years.

JED
Twelve.

She stops. Her hand drifts to the holster.

He raises his glove hand. Slow. Palm out.

JED (CONT'D)
I don't have a gun.

MARISOL
Liar.

JED
Left it in the well.

She stares. Then at the well. Back at him.

MARISOL
Why?

JED
So you'd have to ask.

She draws.

He doesn't move. The barrel shakes. The hawk screams.

Then she holsters it, and slaps him once across the face with the flat of her hand. He takes it.

MARISOL
Now we're even.

She turns for her horse. He lets out a breath that's been twelve years in the making.
